  "result": "At the edge of a small town, there was a library that stayed open after midnight. Its windows glowed amber against the fields, and moths gathered on the glass as if they, too, had questions.\n\nNo one knew why the librarian, Mara, kept the doors unlocked so late. Officially, she said it was for night workers and lonely students. Privately, she believed books behaved differently when the world was quiet.\n\nDuring the day, they sat politely in their rows, offering maps, recipes, histories, and heartbreaks. After midnight, Mara claimed, they began to whisper across the shelves.\n\nShe never tried to prove it. Instead, she brewed tea in a chipped blue pot and listened. Sometimes a travel guide would fall open beside a book of poems. Sometimes an astronomy atlas would appear beneath a gardener\u2019s manual. Mara took these arrangements seriously.\n\nOne rainy November night, a boy arrived wearing a soaked red coat. He asked for a book about leaving. Mara led him to the back room, where the shelves leaned like old friends.\n\n\u201cThere are many,\u201d she said. \u201cBut do you mean leaving a place, a person, or an idea?\u201d\n\nThe boy considered this. Rain tapped the roof with patient fingers. \u201cI don\u2019t know,\u201d he admitted.\n\nMara nodded and handed him a thin novel about a sailor who returned home changed. \u201cThen start here,\u201d she said.\n\nHe read until dawn. When he left, the rain had stopped, and the library windows held the pale morning like a promise not yet explained fully.",
